#!/bin/sh
#
# Validate and report the architecture decisions in design/decisions/ (one file per decision, with
# README.md as the index, whose table this script generates).
#
# Why this exists: 2,000-odd `§N` citations across the kernel, the crates, the notes and the roadmap
# point into this record, ~400 of them from code comments, and a citation of a section that does not
# exist is a dangling footnote in the one place this project asks a reader to trust prose. Two
# concurrent milestones both claimed §30 on 2026-07-30 and it was caught only because a person
# noticed.
#
# Why a directory instead of the old single DECISIONS.md (milestone 114 (split `DECISIONS.md`, and give a decision a status)): the same case milestone 76 (split the roadmap: one file per milestone)
# made for the roadmap, with nearly the same numbers. The file reached 5,320 lines and 67 sections,
# it took three section-number collisions in one day, and it took a structural defect this script
# could not see. That defect is worth naming, because §61 (a lint is adopted on evidence from this tree, not on its description) predicted it and the prediction is what
# got damaged: commit 255879e appended §67 (a program's second stream is a declaration, not a number) twice, once correctly and once into the MIDDLE of §61's
# BUGS sentence, because the insertion anchored on the string `## Reading` and the first occurrence
# of that string in the file was a wrapped prose line inside §61 saying that this script "cannot see
# a section in the WRONG PLACE, only a missing number". Every gate reported clean for a day.
#
# An earlier version of this header argued AGAINST splitting, on the grounds that a duplicate number
# in one file is a text conflict a human sees, where `30-dma.md` and `30-c-seam.md` would merge clean
# and silently. That argument was right about the risk and wrong about the remedy: the collision is
# checkable, and it is checked below. Recorded rather than deleted, because the reasoning is the
# record.
#
# ---- why the status is a field and the index is generated (milestone 582) -----------------------
#
# Until 2026-09-23 a decision's status was `**Status: DECIDED.**`, a data field written as a
# sentence, and this script read it with `re.match(r"\*\*Status: (.+?)\.\*\*", first)`. Two failures
# on record come from exactly that shape. A file said `**Status: NOT YET`, the regex captured `NOT`,
# dropped `YET`, and the report printed a word nobody had written; that is §211 (what a fatal-risk verdict says, and what the chart can plot as a result)'s finding.
# `design/fatal-risks.md`'s risk 7 carried two status lines eighteen lines apart, and the first
# match per section won, so `AUDITED` was invisible to every consumer for weeks.
#
# A schema removes that class of defect. A tighter regex removes the two instances. That is rung one
# of AGENTS.md's ladder against rung two, and the ladder says take the higher rung where it fits.
#
# The schema was ratified by calef on 2026-09-23. Extending it is an architect's call, and nothing
# here extends it:
#
#     ---
#     status: DECIDED          PROPOSED | DECIDED | AMENDED | SUPERSEDED, always
#     raised: 2026-09-23       UTC, always
#     decided: 2026-09-23      UTC, required by DECIDED and AMENDED
#     ratified_by: calef       a GitHub username listed in ARCHITECTS.md, required by DECIDED and
#                              AMENDED
#     superseded_by: 42        a section number, required by SUPERSEDED
#     ---
#
# `superseded_by` is its own key rather than half of a compound `SUPERSEDED BY 42` token, which is
# what makes the forward pointer checkable: it resolves to a section or the gate fails.
#
# **`ratified_by` must name somebody ARCHITECTS.md lists** (2026-09-25, UTC, when calef asked that
# the
# tree stop assuming it has one architect). Before, the key accepted any string, which was harmless
# while the only value anyone wrote was `calef` and stops being harmless the day a second name is
# possible: a typo, or a ratification by someone who does not hold the role, would pass. The list is
# read from ARCHITECTS.md's table on every run rather than copied here, so adding an architect is
# one
# row in one file and this gate follows. A removed architect's past ratifications would then fail,
# which is deliberate and not yet exercised: the row is the grant, so removing one is a decision
# that
# should have to say what happens to the record, rather than one this script makes quietly.
#
# **The index table is generated from the files**, which removes the tree's last additive-index
# conflict of the shape milestone 294 (`design/roadmap/README.md`'s index is generated, not hand-maintained) removed from the roadmap. Every lane minting a section had to
# add a row to one sorted file, so every lane in flight collided with every other one, there,
# always. `briefs/rebase-onto-main.md` names it as case 4's worked example, and that example is now
# stale. The prose around the table is NOT generated and must not be: the preamble argues, and only
# the rows are data.
#
# **The files are the authority.** The index-versus-file comparison this script used to run is gone,
# because it can no longer fail: there is one record and the table is a rendering of it. That is
# rung one where the check was rung two, the same move `script/roadmap` recorded when its own table
# became derived.
#
# What is checked, and which defect each check answers:
#   - every file carries frontmatter whose keys are in the schema and whose dates are ISO and UTC;
#   - the required-when rules, against a ratchet (see PROVENANCE GAPS below);
#   - `superseded_by` names a decision that exists, so the pointer out of a retired decision is as
#     checked as a pointer into one;
#   - the prose does not restate a field, because two copies of one fact in one file is the defect
#     the schema was ratified to remove;
#   - each file holds exactly one decision: one H1, first thing after the frontmatter, whose number
#     matches the filename (the §67-inside-§61 class, and the appended-past-the-closer class);
#   - two files claiming one number is fatal, which is the collision the old single file made visible
#     by accident and this makes visible on purpose;
#   - a gap in the numbering is REPORTED and does not fail (milestone 443 (lanes wait on each other for three reasons, and none of them is the work)): the defect is a
#     duplicate, which stays fatal, and failing on a gap is what forced the renumber that moves
#     every citation. The reasoning is at the check.
#   - the committed index matches the one generated from the files;
#   - every `§N` citation TREE-WIDE resolves to a decision (vendor/, patches/ and lockfiles
#     excluded: other people's text and generated files).
#
# ---- where a date comes from ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `raised` is required always, and `decided` and `ratified_by` are required by DECIDED and AMENDED.
# The corpus predating the schema states none of them for most of its files, so milestone 582 (a
# decision's status becomes a field, and the index becomes generated) filled them from two sources
# and a rule for choosing between them.
#
# **The rule: take the earlier of what the file says and what the repository shows.** The prose
# supplied 59 raise dates and 106 decision dates; git supplied the other 152 and 62. Prose written
# before 2026-09-13 dates by calef's local day rather than UTC, so where the two disagree it is
# usually the prose that is a day early, and the earlier is the one the file already states.
#
# `raised` is the first commit that wrote the decision down. For the 73 sections that predate
# milestone 114 (split `DECISIONS.md`, and give a decision a status) that is the first commit whose
# `DECISIONS.md` (or `design/open-decisions.md`, for the six it absorbed) carries the heading, matched by
# title and number. After the split it is the file's first commit, found with `--follow` so a
# renumbered or promoted file keeps its origin, merged with a plain walk because `--follow`
# truncates at some renames. `decided` is the first commit whose blob reads DECIDED or AMENDED, in
# either spelling; a section filed into `DECISIONS.md` was filed decided, so for those it is the
# raise commit. **Author date, in UTC**, like every other date in this tree.
#
# **A git date is the date, not an upper bound.** calef, 2026-09-24: "We write things down when we
# raise them. There is no gap between the commit and when it was raised." The split commit's date,
# 2026-08-04, is when files were cut and dates no decision; a first pass used it and was wrong.
#
# Fifteen files came out raised the day after they were decided, and `raised` is clamped to
# `decided` there. Twelve are a Pacific evening commit rolling over into the next UTC day. Three
# (§28 (SMP placement), §34 (RedoxFS is the primary filesystem), §46 (thin primitives or whole
# subsystems)) say in their own prose that they were decided the day before they were written up.
#
# **`ratified_by` is `calef` on all 168**, on his own ruling of 2026-09-24: he is the only ratifier
# to date. An earlier pass of this migration filled the same 168 by inference and a reviewer was
# right to refuse it, because a lane inferring the architect's role from the constitution is not the
# same act as the architect stating it. The value is identical and its provenance is not.
#
# **There is no gap ledger and the required-when rules are hard failures.** There was one, briefly:
# `design/decisions/PROVENANCE-GAPS.md`, a ratchet carrying 302 entries, which is what this check
# needed while the corpus held facts nobody had written down. Deriving the dates emptied it, and an
# allowlist with nothing on it is weaker than no allowlist, so it went. A missing required key now
# fails, which is rung one where the ledger was rung two.
#
# BUGS
#   The restatement check cannot refuse an attributed sentence that happens to repeat a date the
#   frontmatter holds. `calef, 2026-09-18: <ruling>` is the shape, 130 files use it, and it is voice
#   rather than a duplicated field. What is refused is the field-shaped forms: a `**Status: ...**`
#   token anywhere outside a code fence, and an opening sentence of `Ratified by <user> on <date>`
#   or `Decided <date>`. A gate that also stripped the attributed form would make 130 files worse to
#   read and would remove no ambiguity, since the frontmatter is the field either way.
#
#   Nothing checks that a `raised` date precedes its `decided` date, or that either is a date this
#   project existed on. Both are cheap to add and neither has yet been wrong.
#
#   A citation that RESOLVES but points where its author did not mean is still invisible, and the
#   tree has already produced two of them; after any renumber, check by content and not by running
#   this. **Milestone 97 (citations that name what they cite) is the milestone that closes it** (a citation names what it cites, and the
#   name is compared against the target). Milestone 114 exists partly to make 97 cheap: now that a
#   decision is a file with a title on its own line, `§43 (reading the clock is a page)` can be
#   checked against `43-clock-authority.md`'s own H1 without parsing a 5,000-line document.
#
#   Row title == file title is not checked and does not need to be: the row IS the file title now.
#
#   script/decisions               print the index, grouped by status
#   script/decisions --check       validate; non-zero on a problem (wired into script/lint)
#   script/decisions --index       print README.md with the table regenerated from the files
#   script/decisions --write-index write that back
#
# Name: recorded (DECISIONS §134, which writes the `script/` family's split down). Introduced
# 2026-07-30 with the decision index. §134 states the rule nobody had recorded until then: what
# *does* something in this directory takes a verb (`verify`, `test`, `fuzz`, `bench`) and what
# *reports* takes a noun, and it lists this script by name in the noun half beside `names`,
# `citations`, `roadmap`, `coverage` and `mutation`. The stem raises no second question, because
# the noun is the record it reads. no architect has ratified it.

set -e

ROOT="$(CDPATH='' cd -- "$(dirname -- "$0")/.." && pwd)"

exec python3 - "$ROOT" "${1:-}" <<'PY'
import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys

root, mode = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2]
d = os.path.join(root, "design", "decisions")
index_path = os.path.join(d, "README.md")

# The lifecycle, in the order a decision travels it. See design/decisions/README.md.
VOCAB = ["PROPOSED", "DECIDED", "AMENDED", "SUPERSEDED"]
KEYS = ["status", "raised", "decided", "ratified_by", "superseded_by"]
DATE_KEYS = ["raised", "decided"]
DATE = re.compile(r"\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}")
FNAME = re.compile(r"(\d+)-[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]*\.md")

problems = []


def architects():
    """The GitHub usernames ARCHITECTS.md's table lists, or None when the file cannot be read.

    The table's first column is the username, one row per architect. A header row, the separator
    row and anything after the table are not rows of it.
    """
    try:
        text = open(os.path.join(root, "ARCHITECTS.md"), encoding="utf-8").read()
    except OSError:
        return None
    names, in_table = set(), False
    for line in text.split("\n"):
        if not line.startswith("|"):
            if in_table:
                break
            continue
        cells = [c.strip() for c in line.strip().strip("|").split("|")]
        if not in_table:
            in_table = cells[0].lower() == "github username"
            continue
        if set(cells[0]) <= set("-: "):
            continue
        names.add(cells[0].strip("`"))
    return names or None


ARCHITECTS = architects()
if ARCHITECTS is None:
    problems.append("ARCHITECTS.md: no table with a 'GitHub username' column, so no 'ratified_by' can "
                    "be checked. That file lists who holds the architect role; this gate reads it")
    ARCHITECTS = set()


def parse_frontmatter(fname, lines):
    """({key: value}, index of the first line after the closing fence), or (None, 0)."""
    if not lines or lines[0] != "---":
        problems.append(f"{fname}: no frontmatter. A decision opens with '---', the schema's keys "
                        f"one per line, and '---' (milestone 582; the schema is in this script's "
                        f"header and in design/decisions/README.md)")
        return None, 0
    try:
        end = lines.index("---", 1)
    except ValueError:
        problems.append(f"{fname}: the frontmatter is never closed by a second '---'")
        return None, 0
    fm = {}
    for n, line in enumerate(lines[1:end], start=2):
        m = re.fullmatch(r"([a-z_]+): (.+)", line)
        if not m:
            problems.append(f"{fname}:{n}: {line[:40]!r} is not '<key>: <value>'. The frontmatter "
                            f"is flat key-value pairs and nothing else: no lists, no nesting")
            continue
        key, value = m.group(1), m.group(2).strip()
        if key not in KEYS:
            problems.append(f"{fname}:{n}: {key!r} is not in the schema "
                            f"({', '.join(KEYS)}). Extending the schema is an architect's call, "
                            f"not a lane's")
        elif key in fm:
            problems.append(f"{fname}:{n}: {key!r} twice. One fact, one field")
        else:
            fm[key] = value
    return fm, end + 1


def check_frontmatter(fname, fm):
    status = fm.get("status")
    if status is None:
        problems.append(f"{fname}: no 'status' key, which is the one field every decision carries")
    elif status not in VOCAB:
        problems.append(f"{fname}: status {status!r} is not in the vocabulary "
                        f"({', '.join(VOCAB)})")
        status = None
    for key in DATE_KEYS:
        if key in fm and not DATE.fullmatch(fm[key]):
            problems.append(f"{fname}: {key} is {fm[key]!r}, not a YYYY-MM-DD date. Every date in "
                            f"this tree is UTC")
    # Required-when, against the ratchet. A gap already recorded is a worklist item; a new one is a
    # failure, which is what makes the backlog able only to shrink.
    required = ["raised"]
    if status in ("DECIDED", "AMENDED"):
        required += ["decided", "ratified_by"]
    if status == "SUPERSEDED":
        required += ["superseded_by"]
    for key in required:
        if fm.get(key):
            continue
        problems.append(f"{fname}: status is {status} and there is no {key!r}. Every key the schema "
                        f"requires is recoverable: a date from the commit that added the file or "
                        f"turned it {status}, and the ratifier from the decision itself. See this "
                        f"script's header for which source wins when they disagree")
    if fm.get("ratified_by") and ARCHITECTS and fm["ratified_by"] not in ARCHITECTS:
        problems.append(f"{fname}: ratified_by is {fm['ratified_by']!r}, who is not listed in "
                        f"ARCHITECTS.md ({', '.join(sorted(ARCHITECTS))}). Only an architect "
                        f"ratifies, and that file's table is the list")
    for key in ("decided", "ratified_by"):
        if key in fm and status in ("PROPOSED", "SUPERSEDED"):
            problems.append(f"{fname}: status is {status} and it carries {key!r}, which belongs to "
                            f"DECIDED and AMENDED")
    if "superseded_by" in fm:
        if status != "SUPERSEDED":
            problems.append(f"{fname}: status is {status} and it carries 'superseded_by'")
        elif not fm["superseded_by"].isdigit():
            problems.append(f"{fname}: superseded_by is {fm['superseded_by']!r}, not a section "
                            f"number")
    return status


# The field-shaped forms, matched against a line's stripped text.
#
# **Each is anchored at the start of the line on purpose.** `**Status:` where a line begins is the
# field this migration removed; the same token mid-sentence or inside backticks is prose ABOUT a
# status line, which five files legitimately are: §211 quotes `design/fatal-risks.md`'s two
# conflicting lines, §140 (the words a finished milestone may use to say what happened to the work it named) compares a proposal's spelling to a decision's, and §90 (the claim is a draft pull request; the status flip is a gate) names where a
# proposal lives. A check that refused those would be refusing the record of the defect it exists
# to prevent.
RESTATED = [
    (re.compile(r"^\*\*Status:"),
     "a '**Status:' line. The status is the frontmatter's 'status' key, and a second copy is the "
     "defect the schema removed"),
    (re.compile(r"^[Rr]atified by \w+,? on \d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}"),
     "an opening that restates 'decided' and 'ratified_by'. Both are frontmatter keys; the "
     "sentence should say what was decided"),
    (re.compile(r"^[Dd]ecided,? (?:on )?\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}"),
     "an opening that restates 'decided', which is a frontmatter key"),
]


# ---- the files, which are the whole record -----------------------------------
ids = {}
statuses = {}
frontmatter = {}
titles = {}
bodies = {}

for fname in sorted(os.listdir(d)):
    if fname == "README.md":
        continue
    m = FNAME.fullmatch(fname)
    if not m:
        problems.append(f"{fname}: not the index and not a decision file "
                        f"(expected <number>-<slug>.md); nothing else lives here")
        continue
    num = int(m.group(1))
    if num in ids:
        problems.append(f"§{num} is claimed by two files: {ids[num]} and {fname} "
                        f"(one number, one decision, or every citation to it is ambiguous)")
        continue
    ids[num] = fname

    lines = open(os.path.join(d, fname)).read().split("\n")
    fm, body = parse_frontmatter(fname, lines)
    if fm is None:
        continue
    frontmatter[num] = fm
    statuses[num] = check_frontmatter(fname, fm)

    while body < len(lines) and not lines[body].strip():
        body += 1
    h1 = re.match(rf"# {num}\. (\S.*)$", lines[body] if body < len(lines) else "")
    if not h1:
        problems.append(f"{fname}: the first line after the frontmatter must be '# {num}. <title>' "
                        f"(found {(lines[body] if body < len(lines) else '')[:40]!r})")
        continue
    titles[num] = h1.group(1).strip()
    bodies[num] = body

# Pass two, which needs every title first: a heading that repeats ANOTHER decision's title is that
# decision arriving somewhere it does not live. A number alone does not prove it, because §41 (the endpoint is the broker, and a device is revoked by taking it back)
# legitimately runs "## 1." to "## 4." over the four things building milestone 23 (a capability-routed component OS with live replacement) settled.
by_title = {t: n for n, t in titles.items()}
for num, fname in sorted(ids.items()):
    if num not in bodies:
        continue
    lines = open(os.path.join(d, fname)).read().split("\n")
    body = bodies[num]
    fence = False
    first_prose = True
    for n, line in enumerate(lines[body + 1:], start=body + 2):
        if line.lstrip().startswith("```"):
            fence = not fence
            continue
        if fence:
            continue
        if line.startswith("# "):
            problems.append(f"{fname}:{n}: a second H1: one file is one decision, and appended "
                            f"text belongs under it, not beside it")
        h = re.match(r"#{1,6} (\d+)\. (.+)$", line)
        if h and by_title.get(h.group(2).strip()) not in (None, num):
            problems.append(f"{fname}:{n}: §{h.group(1)}'s heading ({h.group(2)[:40]!r}) inside "
                            f"§{num}'s file: one file is one decision")
        for pattern, why in RESTATED:
            # The two date forms are refused only where the prose opens, because that is where a
            # field restatement sits; a date in the argument below is argument.
            if pattern.pattern != r"^\*\*Status:" and not first_prose:
                continue
            if pattern.match(line.strip()):
                problems.append(f"{fname}:{n}: the prose carries {why}")
        if line.strip():
            first_prose = False

# `superseded_by` must point somewhere. The retired decision is kept, never deleted, so its forward
# pointer is the only way a reader gets from it to what replaced it.
for num, fm in frontmatter.items():
    target = fm.get("superseded_by", "")
    if target.isdigit() and int(target) not in ids:
        problems.append(f"§{num} says it is superseded by §{target}, which does not exist")

if not ids:
    problems.append("no decision files found: has the directory moved?")

# A gap is REPORTED and does not fail (milestone 443). It used to be a problem, on the reasoning
# that a gap is almost always a renumber on merge that left a hole nobody meant to leave, and that
# reasoning had the cost backwards.
#
# **A duplicate is the defect; a gap is the cure's side effect.** Two files claiming §156 (what the package manager waits on) makes
# every citation to it ambiguous, which is why that stays fatal. A hole at §157 (a trivial install is a web page, a USB drive, and packages over the internet) misleads nobody:
# `§157` appears nowhere, every citation that exists still resolves, and the number line is not a
# promise that it is dense.
#
# **Failing on the gap is what forced the renumber, and the renumber is the expensive thing.** On
# 2026-09-19 two sessions collided on §156 and §158 (a program is declared once: the archives read `Cargo.toml`) in one day. Whoever lands second has to change
# the file, its index row, its H1, its own status and every `§N` in the tree that points at it, and
# a renumber is exactly the operation this script's own header says it cannot check the meaning of.
# So the gate was spending the tree's most citation-dangerous edit to keep a property no reader uses.
#
# **What this can no longer catch**, stated plainly because it is a real loss: a decision file
# deleted outright, where nothing in the tree cites it. Every other shape still fires, including a
# citation to a number that has no file, which is the shape a half-finished deletion actually takes.
#
# A withdrawn proposal still does not make a gap: it stays, as the record of what was declined.
numbers = sorted(ids)
gap_numbers = sorted(set(range(1, numbers[-1] + 1)) - set(numbers)) if numbers else []

# ---- the index, rendered from the files ---------------------------------------
# The region is anchored on the `## The decisions` heading and ends after the rows, rather than on
# HTML comment markers. A marker is a second thing to keep correct, and the heading is already
# load-bearing: a reader navigates by it and GitHub renders it.
TABLE_HEADING = "## The decisions"


def row_status(num):
    """What the table shows. `SUPERSEDED BY N` is reassembled from the two keys that replaced it,
    because a row that said only SUPERSEDED would drop the pointer a reader follows."""
    status = statuses.get(num)
    if status == "SUPERSEDED":
        return f"SUPERSEDED BY {frontmatter[num].get('superseded_by', '?')}"
    return status or ""


def render_index(text):
    """Return `text` with the decisions table replaced by one generated from the files."""
    lines = text.split("\n")
    try:
        start = lines.index(TABLE_HEADING)
    except ValueError:
        return None
    n = start + 1
    while n < len(lines) and not lines[n].startswith("| "):
        n += 1
    end = n
    while end < len(lines) and lines[end].startswith("|"):
        end += 1
    body = ["| # | Status | Decision |", "|---|---|---|"]
    for num in sorted(ids):
        if num not in statuses or num not in titles:
            continue
        title = titles[num].replace("|", "\\|")
        body.append(f"| {num} | {row_status(num)} | [{title}]({ids[num]}) |")
    return "\n".join(lines[:n] + body + lines[end:])



try:
    index = open(index_path).read()
except FileNotFoundError:
    print(f"decisions: {index_path} not found", file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(1)

generated_index = render_index(index)
if generated_index is None:
    problems.append(f"the index has no '{TABLE_HEADING}' heading, so the table cannot be generated")

if mode == "--index":
    if generated_index is None:
        sys.exit(1)
    print(generated_index, end="")
    sys.exit(0)

if mode == "--write-index":
    if generated_index is None:
        sys.exit(1)
    open(index_path, "w").write(generated_index)
    sys.exit(0)

if generated_index is not None and generated_index != index:
    problems.append("the index table does not match the files it is generated from; run "
                    "`script/decisions --write-index`")

# ---- citations, tree-wide ----------------------------------------------------
# **`git grep` output is not only matches.** For a binary file it prints the SENTENCE
# "Binary file <path> matches" instead, so this stream can contain prose about files rather than
# text from them. Feeding that to `int()` is how this check died three times on 2026-08-02 with
# `invalid literal for int() with base 10: 'inary'`: the tokeniser took "Binary", stripped one
# character for the section sign, and tried to read the rest as a number.
#
# The cause each time was a committed build artifact, and the diagnosis cost far more than the
# artifact did, because the error named a string that appears nowhere in the tree. So parse only
# what the pattern promised, and say plainly when the stream contains something else: a checker
# that cannot read its own input should report that, not crash inside a conversion.
refs = subprocess.run(
    ["git", "grep", "-hoE", "§[0-9]+", "--", ":!design/decisions", ":!vendor", ":!patches",
     ":!*.lock"],
    cwd=root, capture_output=True, text=True,
).stdout

cited = set()
unparsed = []
for token in refs.split():
    if token.startswith("§") and token[1:].isdigit():
        cited.add(int(token[1:]))
    else:
        unparsed.append(token)
if unparsed:
    joined = " ".join(unparsed[:12])
    print(f"decisions: ignoring {len(unparsed)} unparseable git-grep token(s): {joined}",
          file=sys.stderr)
    print("decisions: this usually means a binary file is tracked (git grep prints a sentence, "
          "not a match). Check `git ls-files` for build artifacts.", file=sys.stderr)
for n in sorted(cited - set(ids)):
    problems.append(f"§{n} is cited in the tree but no decision has that number")

# ---- report ------------------------------------------------------------------
if mode == "--check":
    for p in sorted(set(problems)):
        print(f"decisions: {p}", file=sys.stderr)
    if problems:
        sys.exit(1)
    print(f"decisions: {len(ids)} decisions, frontmatter valid and complete, index generated from "
          f"the files, every tree-wide citation resolves")
    if gap_numbers:
        print(f"decisions: {len(gap_numbers)} unused number"
              f"{'s' if len(gap_numbers) != 1 else ''} "
              f"({', '.join('§' + str(n) for n in gap_numbers)}). Reported, not a failure: a "
              f"duplicate is the defect, a gap is not, and renumbering to close one moves "
              f"citations")
    sys.exit(0)

by_status = {}
for num in sorted(ids):
    if num in statuses and num in titles:
        by_status.setdefault(statuses[num], []).append(num)

for status in VOCAB:
    items = by_status.get(status, [])
    if not items:
        continue
    print(f"\n{status} ({len(items)})")
    for n in items:
        tag = ""
        if status == "SUPERSEDED":
            tag = f" -> §{frontmatter[n].get('superseded_by', '?')}"
        print(f"  §{n:<3} {titles[n][:72]}{tag}")

print(f"\ntotal: {len(ids)} decisions")
if gap_numbers:
    print(f"unused numbers: {', '.join('§' + str(n) for n in gap_numbers)}")
for p in sorted(set(problems)):
    print(f"PROBLEM: {p}")
